ool), activation refused when the encoded CA DN list exceeds the 65,535-byte CertificateRequest bound, ServerName = the expected UUID-derived DNS SAN HOST_UUID.host.ax.invalid of the selected configured destination (never the SSH endpoint), VerifyConnection supplementing (never replacing) standard chain/name/time/EKU verification with the exact enrolled leaf/root/key/UUID/role/current-trust-state checks from hosttrust, 10-second handshake deadline and 1 MiB incoming handshake byte cap per side enforced even when the peer sends nothing, no application bytes interpreted before local handshake success, hello with its own 10-second deadline and the 8 MiB line / 5 MiB object limits, server finishing verification of the client's Certificate/CertificateVerify/Finished before parsing hello and sending hello success only after current authorization (hosttrust.AuthorizeDispatch with the current committed generation) and identity equality pass (every received hello host ID equals the uniquely verified enrolled UUID; on the client also equals the selected configured destination; local outgoing hello uses the local credential UUID equal to the Config-4 host ID), the initiator validating hello success before any other operation, dispatch and every mutation boundary under the current authorization generation (WithMutationAuthorization), TLS exporters/key logs/secret material never persisted; refusal semantics exactly as written: at most one encrypted Error 1.3.0 failure then close for a non-hello request before hello success or an invalid hello (host_identity_mismatch / peer_not_allowlisted exit 7, incompatible_protocol exit 6 only when incompatibility is actually known), unframeable input and other RPC majors close without a frame, only a TLS alert before TLS completes, initiator-side authentication_failed (7) for known peer certificate/handshake failures, transport_failure (8) for I/O/timeout/unclassified EOF, invalid_config (3) for local config/credential-store loading failure before launch, no new Error enum or exit code; (3) the HC-* gate registry of Section 11.10.4 (HC-TLS, HC-CERT, HC-MAP, HC-HELLO, HC-DISPATCH, HC-MIGRATE, HC-LIFECYCLE, HC-GENERATION, HC-EXCLUDE, HC-PARITY) as executable positive/negative fixture families driven through the production entries — contract model checks, not a deployment claim; Section 11.10.5 is an upstream synthetic source validator and is NOT an AX runtime JSON-policy reader — do not implement a policy reader. Tests: real TLS 1.3 handshakes over net.Pipe or an in-memory duplex with certificates issued through the landed hosttrust issuance path (Section 11.10.2 profile: ECDSA P-256 root + leaf, exact CN/SAN/EKU/validity members), positives (full handshake, hello success, one RPC-5 operation, initiator and responder roles) and the refusal table (TLS 1.2 offer, missing/wrong ALPN, resumption/PSK/0-RTT attempt, plaintext preface, wrong SAN, unenrolled root, revoked leaf, retired entry past retire_at, expired leaf, system-pool root, hello UUID mismatch on either side, client destination mismatch, non-hello before hello, invalid hello, stale authorization generation at dispatch and at a mutation boundary, oversize handshake bytes, silent peer past the handshake deadline, hello past its deadline, oversize hello line/object), each asserting the exact Error class/exit and the close behavior; the seven-row AC accounting of the original task re-derived through the authenticated runtime consumer (previously 0/7 pending this consumer); narrowing mutants per refusal with the restored mutations.py extended (per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits, applied harmless SURVIVED control); crash/idempotency: the channel writes no durable state — state that bound explicitly and prove it (census of writes); conformance matrix mapping every 11.10/11.10.1 clause and every HC gate to a test or a stated bound; README section and LOGBOOK entry with no capability claim beyond what is tested (no ax command exists; the CLI wiring is a 