{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c69b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0addc-f5a7-7d72-82bc-9d078a927129"},"sequence":424,"recorded_at":1780531400000843,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"05ccea39-0df0-456b-a475-42ee00a3a1b7","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"05ccea39-0df0-456b-a475-42ee00a3a1b7","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"05ccea39-0df0-456b-a475-42ee00a3a1b7"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0addd-fc44-7bb3-9948-a3fe0aa4074b"},"task_id":"01a0addd-fc44-7bb3-9948-a3fe0aa4074b","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0addd-fc44-7bb3-9948-a3fe0aa4074b","chunk":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(8006 line(s) above)\n8007|### 11.10 Host Channel 1.0.0 and Mesh RPC 5.0.0\n8008|\n8009|This is the standard cross-platform strong-identity profile, independent of\n8010|SSH implementation. It is a new explicit transport, not an extension to a\n8011|legacy hello. Both endpoints MUST use a maintained TLS implementation; AX MUST\n8012|NOT implement a custom challenge-signature or record-encryption protocol.\n8013|\n8014|#### 11.10.1 Launch, TLS and dispatch\n8015|\n8016|The Config-4 initiator starts exactly the equivalent of:\n8017|\n8018|~~~shell\n8019|ssh -T HOST ax rpc serve --stdio --host-channel 1.0.0\n8020|~~~\n8021|\n8022|SSH remains authenticated and server-verified. OpenSSH over a Tailscale network\n8023|and native Tailscale SSH both carry the same ordered binary stdin/stdout stream;\n8024|no PTY, shell banner on stdout, text conversion, alternate command, or shared\n8025|TCP listener is permitted. Stderr is bounded sanitized diagnostics only.\n8026|Native Tailscale SSH has its own authentication/server and policy; OpenSSH\n8027|<code>authorized_keys</code>/forced-command behavior MUST NOT be assumed for it.\n8028|Neither an SSH username, source address, tailnet node, launcher argument nor\n8029|a hello value establishes AX host identity. Availability of a particular SSH\n8030|server on a platform requires product testing; protocol parity is not a claim\n8031|that native Tailscale SSH servers exist on every AX target.\n8032|\n8033|The initiator is TLS client and remote process TLS server. The first stdout/\n8034|stdin bytes are TLS records, with no plaintext preface or version negotiation.\n8035|Both sides require TLS 1.3 exclusively, ALPN exactly <code>ax-host/1</code>,\n8036|full certificate authentication on every connection, and the Section 11.10.2\n8037|certificate checks. Session tickets, PSKs, session resumption, 0-RTT, TLS 1.2,\n8038|post-handshake client authentication, plaintext fallback and downgrade retries\n8039|are forbidden. A missing ALPN or resumed connection is rejected. There is no\n8040|RPC frame, error envelope, health read, version probe, or application dispatch\n8041|before mutual authentication; failed TLS closes the SSH stream.\n8042|\n8043|In Go, configure <code>MinVersion=MaxVersion=tls.VersionTLS13</code>,\n8044|<code>InsecureSkipVerify=false</code>,\n8045|<code>ClientAuth=tls.RequireAndVerifyClientCert</code> on the server,\n8046|<code>SessionTicketsDisabled=true</code>, a nil client session cache, and\n8047|<code>NextProtos=[\"ax-host/1\"]</code>. Use only the explicitly enrolled roots\n8048|in <code>RootCAs</code>/<code>ClientCAs</code>, never the system pool. Exclude\n8049|revoked/expired entries from these pools. The total encoded CA distinguished-\n8050|name list MUST fit the TLS 65,535-byte CertificateRequest authority-list bound;\n8051|refuse activation if it cannot, never truncate the trust set silently. The client\n8052|sets <code>ServerName</code> to the expected UUID-derived DNS SAN below, not the\n8053|SSH endpoint. Additional <code>VerifyConnection</code> checks MUST supplement\n8054|successful standard chain/name/time/EKU verification, not replace or disable it.\n8055|They check the exact enrolled leaf, root, key, UUID, role and current trust state.\n8056|TLS exporters, key logs and secret traffic material MUST NOT be persisted.\n8057|\n8058|The TLS handshake has a fixed 10-second wall-clock deadline and at most 1 MiB\n8059|of incoming handshake bytes per side, including certificate records, before\n8060|completion; an adapter around SSH pipes MUST enforce cancellation and these\n8061|limits even when a peer sends no bytes. The subsequent hello has a separate\n8062|10-second deadline and the existing 8 MiB line / 5 MiB object limits; remaining\n8063|requests use the configured RPC timeout. Limits count bytes, not characters.\n8064|No application bytes may be interpreted as RPC until the TLS handshake returns\n8065|success locally. TLS permits a client to finish its handshake before the server\n8066|has accepted its final flight; consequently the client may send only encrypted\n8067|hello at that point. The server MUST finish verifying the client's Certificate,\n8068|CertificateVerify and Finished before parsing hello, and sends hello success\n8069|only after current authorization and identity equality pass. The initiator\n8070|MUST receive and validate that success before any other RPC operation.\n8071|\n8072|RPC 5 has the exact RPC-4 operations, envelope/body shapes, eight namespaces,\n8073|limits and 25-key hello map from Section 11.9, with only the containing\n8074|<code>protocol_version</code> and <code>contracts.rpc</code> changed to\n8075|<code>5.0.0</code> and <code>[\"5.0.0\"]</code>. No authentication, trust,\n8076|configuration, error or transport key is added to hello. Structured Error\n8077|remains statically bound to 1.3.0. Every received hello host ID MUST equal the\n8078|uniquely verified enrolled host UUID; on the client that UUID MUST also equal\n8079|the selected configured destination. Local outgoing hello MUST use the local\n8080|credential's UUID equal to Config-4 host ID. Nonces retain their correlation\n8081|semantics and are not identity proof. A TLS-authenticated peer is still refused\n8082|when it is not allowlisted or its hello identity differs.\n8083|\n8084|After TLS, a valid RPC-5 non-hello request before hello success or an invalid\n8085|RPC-5 hello receives at most one encrypted Error 1.3.0 failure and closes;\n8086|identity refusal uses <code>host_identity_mismatch</code>, missing allowlist\n8087|permission uses <code>peer_not_allowlisted</code> (both exit 7); a contract\n8088|mismatch <code>incompatible_protocol</code>. Unframeable input and other RPC\n8089|majors close without a frame. Before TLS completes, only a standard TLS alert\n8090|may be sent, never JSON. The initiating CLI uses existing local <code>authentication_failed</code>\n8091|(exit 7) for a known peer certificate/handshake authentication failure and\n8092|<code>transport_failure</code> (exit 8) for transport I/O, timeout or unclassified\n8093|EOF. Local config/credential-store loading failure is <code>invalid_config</code>\n8094|(exit 3) before launch. Use <code>incompatible_protocol</code> (exit 6) only when\n8095|incompatibility is actually known, not inferred from EOF. No new Error enum or\n8096|exit code is introduced.\n8097|\n8098|#### 11.10.2 Certificate issuance and enrolled trust\n8099|\n8100|Host Credential Profile 1 is part of Host Channel 1, with no online CA service.\n8101|For each credential generation the host generates two distinct CSPRNG ECDSA\n8102|P-256 key pairs locally: a self-signed root CA and a leaf signed by that CA.\n8103|Root and leaf use X.509 v3, ECDSA-with-SHA256 signatures and distinct positive\n8104|random 128-bit serial numbers. The root is used only to sign this one leaf;\n8105|its private key MUST be destroyed after successful issuance. Rotation generates\n8106|both fresh keys and certificates. Reissuing a leaf under an enrolled CA does\n8107|not enroll it. TLS sends only the leaf; the exact root is already local trust.\n8108|\n8109|| Certificate member | Root | Leaf |\n8110|| --- | --- | --- |\n8111|| Subject / issuer | CN <code>AX Host Root HOST_UUID</code>, self-issued | CN <code>AX Host HOST_UUID</code>, issuer equals root subject |\n8112|| Basic Constraints (critical) | CA=true, pathLen=0 | CA=false, no pathLen |\n8113|| Key Usage (critical) | keyCertSign only | digitalSignature only |\n8114|| Extended Key Usage (noncritical) | Absent | Exactly clientAuth and serverAuth |\n8115|| Subject Alternative Name (noncritical) | Absent | Exactly one DNS name <code>HOST_UUID.host.ax.invalid</code>; no other SAN type |\n8116|| Validity | notBefore=issuance time minus 300s; notAfter=notBefore plus 366 days | Same notBefore; notAfter=notBefore plus 90 days |\n8117|| Subject Key Identifier (noncritical) | SHA-1 of subjectPublicKey BIT STRING contents | Same construction for leaf key |\n8118|| Authority Key Identifier (noncritical) | Root SKI | Root SKI |\n8119|\n8120|HOST_UUID is the configured lowercase canonical UUIDv7, without braces. No\n8121|wildcard, CN-only name, alternative UUID, extra subject attribute, extension,\n8122|intermediate, AIA fetch, CRL URL or OCSP dependency is allowed in this profile.\n8123|The DNS SAN is a verification name, never a DNS lookup or network endpoint.\n8124|Root self-signature, leaf signature, exact fields above, valid P-256 public\n8125|points, root/leaf lifetime and current validity of both certificates MUST be\n8126|checked at enrollment and handshake. Certificate validity uses the actual\n8127|local UTC time inclusively within notBefore/notAfter; no extra acceptance skew\n8128|or expiry grace is allowed. Unavailable/untrusted local time refuses admission;\n8129|clock correction cannot extend a previously scheduled stream expiry. No FIPS\n8130|certification or hardware non-exportability is claimed by choosing P-256.\n8131|\n8132|Host Trust Store 1.0.0 is owner-only JSON at\n8133|<code>STATE_DIR/host-channel/trust.json</code>. It is machine-local authority,\n8134|not an immutable mesh object, and has exactly these fields:\n8135|\n8136|| Field | Type / constraint |\n8137|| --- | --- |\n8138|| <code>schema</code> | Exact <code>urn:ax:schema:host-trust-store</code> |\n8139|| <code>schema_version</code> | Exact <code>1.0.0</code> |\n8140|| <code>generation</code> | uint53 greater than zero; increments by one per committed trust/config authorization change; exhaustion refuses mutation |\n8141|| <code>entries</code> | CredentialEntry[0..4096], sorted by credential_id, no duplicates |\n8142|\n8143|Each CredentialEntry contains exactly <code>host_id:UUIDv7</code>,\n8144|<code>credential_id:digest</code> (SHA-256 of leaf DER),\n8145|<code>spki_id:digest</code> (SHA-256 of leaf SubjectPublicKeyInfo DER),\n8146|<code>root_id:digest</code> (SHA-256 of root DER),\n8147|<code>leaf_der:base64url</code>, <code>root_der:base64url</code> (each unpadded,\n8148|canonical and decoding to 1..16384 bytes),\n8149|<code>state:active|retiring|revoked</code>, <code>enrolled_at:timestamp</code>,\n8150|and <code>retire_at:timestamp|null</code>. The last field is non-null exactly\n8151|for retiring entries and later than enrolled_at. Revoked entries retain their\n8152|public bytes permanently as reuse tombstones. An unreadable, malformed,\n8153|partially read, duplicate-key, unknown-field or missing store is never an empty\n8154|store. Initial creation of an empty generation-1 store is explicit local setup.\n8155|\n8156|Enrollment is local operator authorization of the tuple (host UUID, leaf DER\n8157|fingerprint, SPKI fingerprint, root DER fingerprint), verified over an\n8158|independent authenticated out-of-band channel. Imported public bytes MUST\n8159|reproduce those fingerprints and the profile before the transaction commits.\n8160|SSH discovery, a first hello, a remote claim, and a certificate being self-signed\n8161|are not approval. Remote RPC cannot mutate the trust store. Both directions\n8162|need enrollment; one side enrolling another does not imply reciprocity.\n8163|\n8164|Across all entries, including revoked ones, a leaf, leaf public key or root\n8165|MUST NOT map to more than one host UUID. Multiple entries for the same leaf or\n8166|SPKI are invalid, including attempted renewal with the same key. Duplicate\n8167|root IDs are also invalid, even for the same UUID. At most two\n8168|non-revoked credentials may belong to a host, solely during rotation. A root\n8169|trusted for one host never grants its other issued certificates admission.\n8170|After ordinary TLS verification, an exact leaf/root/SPKI match against one\n8171|currently admitted entry yields the verified UUID. Zero or multiple matches\n8172|refuse. SAN alone and CA membership alone are insufficient. Both EKUs are\n8173|required by the profile; chain verification additionally validates serverAuth\n8174|at the client and clientAuth at the server.\n8175|\n8176|Local leaf PEM and PKCS#8 private-key PEM are stored under\n8177|<code>STATE_DIR/host-channel/credentials/HEX_CREDENTIAL_ID/</code> as\n8178|<code>certificate.pem</code> and <code>private-key.pem</code>; HEX_CREDENTIAL_ID\n8179|is the 64 lowercase hex characters after <code>sha256:</code>. The root public\n8180|PEM is <code>root.pem</code>. No other file may select identity. Validate the\n8181|private/public match, profile and configured host ID before use. Directories\n8182|are 0700 and files 0600 on Unix; Windows requires equivalent owner-only ACLs.\n8183|Reject symlinks/reparse escapes or group/world write access. Do not forward SSH\n8184|agents or reuse SSH/provider keys. Credential/trust files, backups, private\n8185|keys and authorization caches MUST NOT enter replication, snapshots, cloning,\n8186|Session Directory, logs or exported diagnostic bundles. Operators exchange only\n8187|explicitly selected public enrollment material outside replication.\n8188|\n8189|#### 11.10.3 Rotation, revocation and authorization generations\n8190|\n8191|Rotation keeps host UUID and creates a new credential. Explicitly enroll the\n8192|new tuple on each participating peer before selecting it in Config-4. The\n8193|local atomic trust transition admits the new entry as active and marks the old\n8194|entry retiring with a fixed retire_at no more than 24 hours after that\n8195|transition and no later than old leaf expiry. There is no overlap extension;\n8196|no second rotation starts until the retiring entry is revoked. Failed or\n8197|incomplete enrollment leaves the new route unavailable, never auto-trusted.\n8198|At retire_at old admission ends and old streams close even if the new route\n8199|is unavailable. A peer offline during rotation must explicitly catch up; no\n8200|mesh-wide atomic enrollment or central CA availability is assumed.\n8201|\n8202|Trust changes and Config-4 peer/credential changes share a machine-local\n8203|cross-process authorization lock and crash-durable generation transaction.\n8204|Readers MUST obtain one coherent committed snapshot (config plus trust), never\n8205|mix an old allowlist with new credentials. Every opened stream binds the local\n8206|credential ID, verified remote credential ID/UUID and snapshot generation.\n8207|A trust/config commit invalidates all streams of the prior generation; every\n8208|process, including daemonless RPC children, MUST close them within one second\n8209|of the commit using cancellation plus a bounded watchdog, even when idle.\n8210|Missing/failed generation reads close the stream. Filesystem notifications\n8211|alone are insufficient. At certificate expiry or retire_at the same rule\n8212|applies, with no new dispatch at or after the deadline. Process crashes close\n8213|the underlying stream; a dead process is not an authorization subscriber.\n8214|\n8215|Every application dispatch checks current generation, credential validity,\n8216|allowlist membership and authenticated hello. Its check and dispatch admission\n8217|MUST hold the same authorization lock, so revocation cannot commit between them.\n8218|A read admitted before revocation may be interrupted by stream cancellation;\n8219|no new read is admitted afterward from the stale generation. Each mutation, including resumed\n8220|transfer, retry, queued work and recovery, additionally rechecks these facts\n8221|under the authorization lock immediately before each externally visible\n8222|side-effect boundary. The generation check and that boundary serialize with\n8223|revocation/config commit. Work prepared under an old generation cannot gain\n8224|new authority by refreshing a cached number: discard its authorization and\n8225|replan/revalidate through a fresh mutually authenticated connection. Existing\n8226|lease, operation and idempotency checks still apply. An effect already committed\n8227|before revocation is not undone; pending work must stop at the next boundary\n8228|and use the existing recovery journal. Long provider calls must not hold the\n8229|authorization lock across unbounded work; revocation cannot report success\n8230|while an effect boundary still holds prior authorization.\n8231|\n8232|Revocation is an explicit local atomic transition to revoked, increments the\n8233|generation, forbids all future admissions of that key and closes old streams.\n8234|Removing an allowlisted host also increments generation and invalidates streams.\n8235|Success means the durable decision committed; the documented one-second close\n8236|bound does not permit any later dispatch or mutation from the stale generation.\n8237|A crash after commit but before acknowledgements cannot restore authority.\n8238|Failed commits report failure; they cannot be reported as absence or success.\n8239|Revocation is local: the operator must distribute it explicitly to every peer;\n8240|a disconnected peer with stale trust cannot be claimed globally revoked.\n8241|Loss/copy/compromise of a key requires revocation and new out-of-band enrollment,\n8242|not same-key recovery. Host UUID reuse after total trust-state loss requires\n8243|operator reconciliation of every peer, never automatic enrollment.\n8244|\n8245|#### 11.10.4 Assurance and executable fixture bounds\n8246|\n8247|TLS proves possession of an enrolled private key and protects records. It does\n8248|not prove unique physical hardware, current ownership of a Session, or an\n8249|uncompromised operator/account. A copied key authenticates until each verifier\n8250|revokes it. An attacker able to read/replace local AX state, credentials or\n8251|binaries is outside this assurance. Arbitrary shell access to that account\n8252|cannot be made safe by a wrapper. A restricted server-enforced SSH account is\n8253|an alternative deployment hardening measure, not equivalent host evidence and\n8254|not a substitute for this profile. Remote attach/log CLI paths remain their\n8255|existing SSH contracts; they do not acquire RPC-5 host-channel assurance.\n8256|\n8257|The following closed gate registry defines source-level fixture families.\n8258|Each family MUST have positive and negative vectors. These are contract model\n8259|checks, not an executed TLS deployment, private-key custody proof or product\n8260|conformance result. Real certificate-chain, handshake, race/timeout and platform\n8261|acceptance remains required by <code>AC-HOST-001</code>.\n8262|\n8263|| Gate | Predicate required for admission |\n8264|| --- | --- |\n8265|| HC-TLS | Both peers verify full TLS 1.3 and exact ALPN; no resumption or early data |\n8266|| HC-CERT | Standard chain, profile, time and role verification succeed in both directions |\n8267|| HC-MAP | Exact enrolled leaf/root/key maps uniquely to the configured UUID |\n8268|| HC-HELLO | Both hello IDs equal verified UUIDs and nonce/contract/limit validation succeeds |\n8269|| HC-DISPATCH | No application dispatch before mutual TLS and hello success |\n8270|| HC-MIGRATE | Config 4 selects the explicit Host Channel 1 launch and RPC 5 only |\n8271|| HC-LIFECYCLE | Explicit enrollment and bounded fresh-key rotation; revoked keys never reenter |\n8272|| HC-GENERATION | Dispatch and every mutation boundary use the current committed authorization generation |\n8273|| HC-EXCLUDE | Credential and trust material remains local, outside replication |\n8274|| HC-PARITY | The same TLS requirements apply over OpenSSH and native Tailscale SSH |\n8275|\n8276|#### 11.10.5 Executable admission obligations\n8277|\n8278|The following closed JSON policy is normative source for the synthetic admission\n8279|predicates. The public validator reads it from SPEC.md on every invocation;\n8280|there is no independently hard-coded admission model. Every field is required,\n8281|unknown fields refuse, and all field predicates must hold. Predicates apply to\n8282|every invocation, including request, resume, retry, queue and recovery, on both\n8283|SSH transports; client/server facts are separate required obligations. The entrypoint and\n8284|carrier facts are synthetic execution context, not new RPC wire members. A\n8285|conditional predicate cannot grant an exception to another obligation.\n8286|\n8287|Operators are closed: `eq` is type-exact JSON equality; `range` is an inclusive\n8288|integer interval (booleans excluded); `in` is type-exact set membership;\n8289|`equal_field` is type-exact equality with another present field; `singleton_field`\n8290|is exactly a one-element array of that field; `uuid7` requires the canonical\n8291|lowercase UUIDv7 grammar; `all` is conjunction. `when` has exactly `field`, `in`\n8292|and `require`: it applies its predicate only when the named fact is in the given\n8293|set. It exists to make scope changes executable and testable, not to authorize\n8294|narrowing the universal obligations in this revision. Missing or mistyped\n8295|operands, duplicate keys, unsupported operators and unreadable policy refuse.\n8296|\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c69d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0addc-f5a7-7d72-82bc-9d078a927129"},"sequence":425,"recorded_at":1780531400000845,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"05ccea39-0df0-456b-a475-42ee00a3a1b7","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"05ccea39-0df0-456b-a475-42ee00a3a1b7","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"05ccea39-0df0-456b-a475-42ee00a3a1b7"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0addd-fc44-7bb3-9948-a3fe0aa4074b"},"task_id":"01a0addd-fc44-7bb3-9948-a3fe0aa4074b","event":{"kind":"completed","task_id":"01a0addd-fc44-7bb3-9948-a3fe0aa4074b"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c69f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0addc-f5a7-7d72-82bc-9d078a927129"},"sequence":426,"recorded_at":1780531400000847,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"05ccea39-0df0-456b-a475-42ee00a3a1b7","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"05ccea39-0df0-456b-a475-42ee00a3a1b7","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"05ccea39-0df0-456b-a475-42ee00a3a1b7"},"call_id":"call_01a0adddf83877928634d22f3655a62b","text":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(8006 line(s) above)\n8007|### 11.10 Host Channel 1.0.0 and Mesh RPC 5.0.0\n8008|\n8009|This is the standard cross-platform strong-identity profile, independent of\n8010|SSH implementation. It is a new explicit transport, not an extension to a\n8011|legacy hello. Both endpoints MUST use a maintained TLS implementation; AX MUST\n8012|NOT implement a custom challenge-signature or record-encryption protocol.\n8013|\n8014|#### 11.10.1 Launch, TLS and dispatch\n8015|\n8016|The Config-4 initiator starts exactly the equivalent of:\n8017|\n8018|~~~shell\n8019|ssh -T HOST ax rpc serve --stdio --host-channel 1.0.0\n8020|~~~\n8021|\n8022|SSH remains authenticated and server-verified. OpenSSH over a Tailscale network\n8023|and native Tailscale SSH both carry the same ordered binary stdin/stdout stream;\n8024|no PTY, shell banner on stdout, text conversion, alternate command, or shared\n8025|TCP listener is permitted. Stderr is bounded sanitized diagnostics only.\n8026|Native Tailscale SSH has its own authentication/server and policy; OpenSSH\n8027|<code>authorized_keys</code>/forced-command behavior MUST NOT be assumed for it.\n8028|Neither an SSH username, source address, tailnet node, launcher argument nor\n8029|a hello value establishes AX host identity. Availability of a particular SSH\n8030|server on a platform requires product testing; protocol parity is not a claim\n8031|that native Tailscale SSH servers exist on every AX target.\n8032|\n8033|The initiator is TLS client and remote process TLS server. The first stdout/\n8034|stdin bytes are TLS records, with no plaintext preface or version negotiation.\n8035|Both sides require TLS 1.3 exclusively, ALPN exactly <code>ax-host/1</code>,\n8036|full certificate authentication on every connection, and the Section 11.10.2\n8037|certificate checks. Session tickets, PSKs, session resumption, 0-RTT, TLS 1.2,\n8038|post-handshake client authentication, plaintext fallback and downgrade retries\n8039|are forbidden. A missing ALPN or resumed connection is rejected. There is no\n8040|RPC frame, error envelope, health read, version probe, or application dispatch\n8041|before mutual authentication; failed TLS closes the SSH stream.\n8042|\n8043|In Go, configure <code>MinVersion=MaxVersion=tls.VersionTLS13</code>,\n8044|<code>InsecureSkipVerify=false</code>,\n8045|<code>ClientAuth=tls.RequireAndVerifyClientCert</code> on the server,\n8046|<code>SessionTicketsDisabled=true</code>, a nil client session cache, and\n8047|<code>NextProtos=[\"ax-host/1\"]</code>. Use only the explicitly enrolled roots\n8048|in <code>RootCAs</code>/<code>ClientCAs</code>, never the system pool. Exclude\n8049|revoked/expired entries from these pools. The total encoded CA distinguished-\n8050|name list MUST fit the TLS 65,535-byte CertificateRequest authority-list bound;\n8051|refuse activation if it cannot, never truncate the trust set silently. The client\n8052|sets <code>ServerName</code> to the expected UUID-derived DNS SAN below, not the\n8053|SSH endpoint. Additional <code>VerifyConnection</code> checks MUST supplement\n8054|successful standard chain/name/time/EKU verification, not replace or disable it.\n8055|They check the exact enrolled leaf, root, key, UUID, role and current trust state.\n8056|TLS exporters, key logs and secret traffic material MUST NOT be persisted.\n8057|\n8058|The TLS handshake has a fixed 10-second wall-clock deadline and at most 1 MiB\n8059|of incoming handshake bytes per side, including certificate records, before\n8060|completion; an adapter around SSH pipes MUST enforce cancellation and these\n8061|limits even when a peer sends no bytes. The subsequent hello has a separate\n8062|10-second deadline and the existing 8 MiB line / 5 MiB object limits; remaining\n8063|requests use the configured RPC timeout. Limits count bytes, not characters.\n8064|No application bytes may be interpreted as RPC until the TLS handshake returns\n8065|success locally. TLS permits a client to finish its handshake before the server\n8066|has accepted its final flight; consequently the client may send only encrypted\n8067|hello at that point. The server MUST finish verifying the client's Certificate,\n8068|CertificateVerify and Finished before parsing hello, and sends hello success\n8069|only after current authorization and identity equality pass. The initiator\n8070|MUST receive and validate that success before any other RPC operation.\n8071|\n8072|RPC 5 has the exact RPC-4 operations, envelope/body shapes, eight namespaces,\n8073|limits and 25-key hello map from Section 11.9, with only the containing\n8074|<code>protocol_version</code> and <code>contracts.rpc</code> changed to\n8075|<code>5.0.0</code> and <code>[\"5.0.0\"]</code>. No authentication, trust,\n8076|configuration, error or transport key is added to hello. Structured Error\n8077|remains statically bound to 1.3.0. Every received hello host ID MUST equal the\n8078|uniquely verified enrolled host UUID; on the client that UUID MUST also equal\n8079|the selected configured destination. Local outgoing hello MUST use the local\n8080|credential's UUID equal to Config-4 host ID. Nonces retain their correlation\n8081|semantics and are not identity proof. A TLS-authenticated peer is still refused\n8082|when it is not allowlisted or its hello identity differs.\n8083|\n8084|After TLS, a valid RPC-5 non-hello request before hello success or an invalid\n8085|RPC-5 hello receives at most one encrypted Error 1.3.0 failure and closes;\n8086|identity refusal uses <code>host_identity_mismatch</code>, missing allowlist\n8087|permission uses <code>peer_not_allowlisted</code> (both exit 7); a contract\n8088|mismatch <code>incompatible_protocol</code>. Unframeable input and other RPC\n8089|majors close without a frame. Before TLS completes, only a standard TLS alert\n8090|may be sent, never JSON. The initiating CLI uses existing local <code>authentication_failed</code>\n8091|(exit 7) for a known peer certificate/handshake authentication failure and\n8092|<code>transport_failure</code> (exit 8) for transport I/O, timeout or unclassified\n8093|EOF. Local config/credential-store loading failure is <code>invalid_config</code>\n8094|(exit 3) before launch. Use <code>incompatible_protocol</code> (exit 6) only when\n8095|incompatibility is actually known, not inferred from EOF. No new Error enum or\n8096|exit code is introduced.\n8097|\n8098|#### 11.10.2 Certificate issuance and enrolled trust\n8099|\n8100|Host Credential Profile 1 is part of Host Channel 1, with no online CA service.\n8101|For each credential generation the host generates two distinct CSPRNG ECDSA\n8102|P-256 key pairs locally: a self-signed root CA and a leaf signed by that CA.\n8103|Root and leaf use X.509 v3, ECDSA-with-SHA256 signatures and distinct positive\n8104|random 128-bit serial numbers. The root is used only to sign this one leaf;\n8105|its private key MUST be destroyed after successful issuance. Rotation generates\n8106|both fresh keys and certificates. Reissuing a leaf under an enrolled CA does\n8107|not enroll it. TLS sends only the leaf; the exact root is already local trust.\n8108|\n8109|| Certificate member | Root | Leaf |\n8110|| --- | --- | --- |\n8111|| Subject / issuer | CN <code>AX Host Root HOST_UUID</code>, self-issued | CN <code>AX Host HOST_UUID</code>, issuer equals root subject |\n8112|| Basic Constraints (critical) | CA=true, pathLen=0 | CA=false, no pathLen |\n8113|| Key Usage (critical) | keyCertSign only | digitalSignature only |\n8114|| Extended Key Usage (noncritical) | Absent | Exactly clientAuth and serverAuth |\n8115|| Subject Alternative Name (noncritical) | Absent | Exactly one DNS name <code>HOST_UUID.host.ax.invalid</code>; no other SAN type |\n8116|| Validity | notBefore=issuance time minus 300s; notAfter=notBefore plus 366 days | Same notBefore; notAfter=notBefore plus 90 days |\n8117|| Subject Key Identifier (noncritical) | SHA-1 of subjectPublicKey BIT STRING contents | Same construction for leaf key |\n8118|| Authority Key Identifier (noncritical) | Root SKI | Root SKI |\n8119|\n8120|HOST_UUID is the configured lowercase canonical UUIDv7, without braces. No\n8121|wildcard, CN-only name, alternative UUID, extra subject attribute, extension,\n8122|intermediate, AIA fetch, CRL URL or OCSP dependency is allowed in this profile.\n8123|The DNS SAN is a verification name, never a DNS lookup or network endpoint.\n8124|Root self-signature, leaf signature, exact fields above, valid P-256 public\n8125|points, root/leaf lifetime and current validity of both certificates MUST be\n8126|checked at enrollment and handshake. Certificate validity uses the actual\n8127|local UTC time inclusively within notBefore/notAfter; no extra acceptance skew\n8128|or expiry grace is allowed. Unavailable/untrusted local time refuses admission;\n8129|clock correction cannot extend a previously scheduled stream expiry. No FIPS\n8130|certification or hardware non-exportability is claimed by choosing P-256.\n8131|\n8132|Host Trust Store 1.0.0 is owner-only JSON at\n8133|<code>STATE_DIR/host-channel/trust.json</code>. It is machine-local authority,\n8134|not an immutable mesh object, and has exactly these fields:\n8135|\n8136|| Field | Type / constraint |\n8137|| --- | --- |\n8138|| <code>schema</code> | Exact <code>urn:ax:schema:host-trust-store</code> |\n8139|| <code>schema_version</code> | Exact <code>1.0.0</code> |\n8140|| <code>generation</code> | uint53 greater than zero; increments by one per committed trust/config authorization change; exhaustion refuses mutation |\n8141|| <code>entries</code> | CredentialEntry[0..4096], sorted by credential_id, no duplicates |\n8142|\n8143|Each CredentialEntry contains exactly <code>host_id:UUIDv7</code>,\n8144|<code>credential_id:digest</code> (SHA-256 of leaf DER),\n8145|<code>spki_id:digest</code> (SHA-256 of leaf SubjectPublicKeyInfo DER),\n8146|<code>root_id:digest</code> (SHA-256 of root DER),\n8147|<code>leaf_der:base64url</code>, <code>root_der:base64url</code> (each unpadded,\n8148|canonical and decoding to 1..16384 bytes),\n8149|<code>state:active|retiring|revoked</code>, <code>enrolled_at:timestamp</code>,\n8150|and <code>retire_at:timestamp|null</code>. The last field is non-null exactly\n8151|for retiring entries and later than enrolled_at. Revoked entries retain their\n8152|public bytes permanently as reuse tombstones. An unreadable, malformed,\n8153|partially read, duplicate-key, unknown-field or missing store is never an empty\n8154|store. Initial creation of an empty generation-1 store is explicit local setup.\n8155|\n8156|Enrollment is local operator authorization of the tuple (host UUID, leaf DER\n8157|fingerprint, SPKI fingerprint, root DER fingerprint), verified over an\n8158|independent authenticated out-of-band channel. Imported public bytes MUST\n8159|reproduce those fingerprints and the profile before the transaction commits.\n8160|SSH discovery, a first hello, a remote claim, and a certificate being self-signed\n8161|are not approval. Remote RPC cannot mutate the trust store. Both directions\n8162|need enrollment; one side enrolling another does not imply reciprocity.\n8163|\n8164|Across all entries, including revoked ones, a leaf, leaf public key or root\n8165|MUST NOT map to more than one host UUID. Multiple entries for the same leaf or\n8166|SPKI are invalid, including attempted renewal with the same key. Duplicate\n8167|root IDs are also invalid, even for the same UUID. At most two\n8168|non-revoked credentials may belong to a host, solely during rotation. A root\n8169|trusted for one host never grants its other issued certificates admission.\n8170|After ordinary TLS verification, an exact leaf/root/SPKI match against one\n8171|currently admitted entry yields the verified UUID. Zero or multiple matches\n8172|refuse. SAN alone and CA membership alone are insufficient. Both EKUs are\n8173|required by the profile; chain verification additionally validates serverAuth\n8174|at the client and clientAuth at the server.\n8175|\n8176|Local leaf PEM and PKCS#8 private-key PEM are stored under\n8177|<code>STATE_DIR/host-channel/credentials/HEX_CREDENTIAL_ID/</code> as\n8178|<code>certificate.pem</code> and <code>private-key.pem</code>; HEX_CREDENTIAL_ID\n8179|is the 64 lowercase hex characters after <code>sha256:</code>. The root public\n8180|PEM is <code>root.pem</code>. No other file may select identity. Validate the\n8181|private/public match, profile and configured host ID before use. Directories\n8182|are 0700 and files 0600 on Unix; Windows requires equivalent owner-only ACLs.\n8183|Reject symlinks/reparse escapes or group/world write access. Do not forward SSH\n8184|agents or reuse SSH/provider keys. Credential/trust files, backups, private\n8185|keys and authorization caches MUST NOT enter replication, snapshots, cloning,\n8186|Session Directory, logs or exported diagnostic bundles. Operators exchange only\n8187|explicitly selected public enrollment material outside replication.\n8188|\n8189|#### 11.10.3 Rotation, revocation and authorization generations\n8190|\n8191|Rotation keeps host UUID and creates a new credential. Explicitly enroll the\n8192|new tuple on each participating peer before selecting it in Config-4. The\n8193|local atomic trust transition admits the new entry as active and marks the old\n8194|entry retiring with a fixed retire_at no more than 24 hours after that\n8195|transition and no later than old leaf expiry. There is no overlap extension;\n8196|no second rotation starts until the retiring entry is revoked. Failed or\n8197|incomplete enrollment leaves the new route unavailable, never auto-trusted.\n8198|At retire_at old admission ends and old streams close even if the new route\n8199|is unavailable. A peer offline during rotation must explicitly catch up; no\n8200|mesh-wide atomic enrollment or central CA availability is assumed.\n8201|\n8202|Trust changes and Config-4 peer/credential changes share a machine-local\n8203|cross-process authorization lock and crash-durable generation transaction.\n8204|Readers MUST obtain one coherent committed snapshot (config plus trust), never\n8205|mix an old allowlist with new credentials. Every opened stream binds the local\n8206|credential ID, verified remote credential ID/UUID and snapshot generation.\n8207|A trust/config commit invalidates all streams of the prior generation; every\n8208|process, including daemonless RPC children, MUST close them within one second\n8209|of the commit using cancellation plus a bounded watchdog, even when idle.\n8210|Missing/failed generation reads close the stream. Filesystem notifications\n8211|alone are insufficient. At certificate expiry or retire_at the same rule\n8212|applies, with no new dispatch at or after the deadline. Process crashes close\n8213|the underlying stream; a dead process is not an authorization subscriber.\n8214|\n8215|Every application dispatch checks current generation, credential validity,\n8216|allowlist membership and authenticated hello. Its check and dispatch admission\n8217|MUST hold the same authorization lock, so revocation cannot commit between them.\n8218|A read admitted before revocation may be interrupted by stream cancellation;\n8219|no new read is admitted afterward from the stale generation. Each mutation, including resumed\n8220|transfer, retry, queued work and recovery, additionally rechecks these facts\n8221|under the authorization lock immediately before each externally visible\n8222|side-effect boundary. The generation check and that boundary serialize with\n8223|revocation/config commit. Work prepared under an old generation cannot gain\n8224|new authority by refreshing a cached number: discard its authorization and\n8225|replan/revalidate through a fresh mutually authenticated connection. Existing\n8226|lease, operation and idempotency checks still apply. An effect already committed\n8227|before revocation is not undone; pending work must stop at the next boundary\n8228|and use the existing recovery journal. Long provider calls must not hold the\n8229|authorization lock across unbounded work; revocation cannot report success\n8230|while an effect boundary still holds prior authorization.\n8231|\n8232|Revocation is an explicit local atomic transition to revoked, increments the\n8233|generation, forbids all future admissions of that key and closes old streams.\n8234|Removing an allowlisted host also increments generation and invalidates streams.\n8235|Success means the durable decision committed; the documented one-second close\n8236|bound does not permit any later dispatch or mutation from the stale generation.\n8237|A crash after commit but before acknowledgements cannot restore authority.\n8238|Failed commits report failure; they cannot be reported as absence or success.\n8239|Revocation is local: the operator must distribute it explicitly to every peer;\n8240|a disconnected peer with stale trust cannot be claimed globally revoked.\n8241|Loss/copy/compromise of a key requires revocation and new out-of-band enrollment,\n8242|not same-key recovery. Host UUID reuse after total trust-state loss requires\n8243|operator reconciliation of every peer, never automatic enrollment.\n8244|\n8245|#### 11.10.4 Assurance and executable fixture bounds\n8246|\n8247|TLS proves possession of an enrolled private key and protects records. It does\n8248|not prove unique physical hardware, current ownership of a Session, or an\n8249|uncompromised operator/account. A copied key authenticates until each verifier\n8250|revokes it. An attacker able to read/replace local AX state, credentials or\n8251|binaries is outside this assurance. Arbitrary shell access to that account\n8252|cannot be made safe by a wrapper. A restricted server-enforced SSH account is\n8253|an alternative deployment hardening measure, not equivalent host evidence and\n8254|not a substitute for this profile. Remote attach/log CLI paths remain their\n8255|existing SSH contracts; they do not acquire RPC-5 host-channel assurance.\n8256|\n8257|The following closed gate registry defines source-level fixture families.\n8258|Each family MUST have positive and negative vectors. These are contract model\n8259|checks, not an executed TLS deployment, private-key custody proof or product\n8260|conformance result. Real certificate-chain, handshake, race/timeout and platform\n8261|acceptance remains required by <code>AC-HOST-001</code>.\n8262|\n8263|| Gate | Predicate required for admission |\n8264|| --- | --- |\n8265|| HC-TLS | Both peers verify full TLS 1.3 and exact ALPN; no resumption or early data |\n8266|| HC-CERT | Standard chain, profile, time and role verification succeed in both directions |\n8267|| HC-MAP | Exact enrolled leaf/root/key maps uniquely to the configured UUID |\n8268|| HC-HELLO | Both hello IDs equal verified UUIDs and nonce/contract/limit validation succeeds |\n8269|| HC-DISPATCH | No application dispatch before mutual TLS and hello success |\n8270|| HC-MIGRATE | Config 4 selects the explicit Host Channel 1 launch and RPC 5 only |\n8271|| HC-LIFECYCLE | Explicit enrollment and bounded fresh-key rotation; revoked keys never reenter |\n8272|| HC-GENERATION | Dispatch and every mutation boundary use the current committed authorization generation |\n8273|| HC-EXCLUDE | Credential and trust material remains local, outside replication |\n8274|| HC-PARITY | The same TLS requirements apply over OpenSSH and native Tailscale SSH |\n8275|\n8276|#### 11.10.5 Executable admission obligations\n8277|\n8278|The following closed JSON policy is normative source for the synthetic admission\n8279|predicates. The public validator reads it from SPEC.md on every invocation;\n8280|there is no independently hard-coded admission model. Every field is required,\n8281|unknown fields refuse, and all field predicates must hold. Predicates apply to\n8282|every invocation, including request, resume, retry, queue and recovery, on both\n8283|SSH transports; client/server facts are separate required obligations. The entrypoint and\n8284|carrier facts are synthetic execution context, not new RPC wire members. A\n8285|conditional predicate cannot grant an exception to another obligation.\n8286|\n8287|Operators are closed: `eq` is type-exact JSON equality; `range` is an inclusive\n8288|integer interval (booleans excluded); `in` is type-exact set membership;\n8289|`equal_field` is type-exact equality with another present field; `singleton_field`\n8290|is exactly a one-element array of that field; `uuid7` requires the canonical\n8291|lowercase UUIDv7 grammar; `all` is conjunction. `when` has exactly `field`, `in`\n8292|and `require`: it applies its predicate only when the named fact is in the given\n8293|set. It exists to make scope changes executable and testable, not to authorize\n8294|narrowing the universal obligations in this revision. Missing or mistyped\n8295|operands, duplicate keys, unsupported operators and unreadable policy refuse.\n8296|\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
